# The National Movement 1885-1919: From Petitions to Mass Politics

> Between 1885 and 1919 the Indian National Congress grew from a body of
> petitioners into a mass organisation — through moderate economic critique,
> extremist Swadeshi agitation, revolutionary secret societies, the Muslim
> League, and Tilak-Besant Home Rule, until Rowlatt and Gandhi changed the
> rules of the game.

## 1. Before the Congress

Post-1857 associations had prepared the ground:

| Association | Year / Founder |
| --- | --- |
| East India Association | 1866, Dadabhai Naoroji (London) |
| Indian Association | 1876, Surendranath Banerjea and Anand Mohan Bose, Calcutta |
| Poona Sarvajanik Sabha | 1870, Justice M. G. Ranade |
| Madras Mahajana Sabha | 1884 |
| Bombay Presidency Association | 1885, Pherozeshah Mehta and Badruddin Tyabji |

Surendranath's **Indian National Conference** (1883) had already convened;
in 1886 it merged into the Congress.

## 2. The Founding of the Congress

The **Indian National Congress** first met in Bombay on **28 December 1885**
at Gokuldas Tejpal College — **72 delegates**, with **W. C. Bonnerjee** in
the chair and the retired ICS officer **A. O. Hume** as convener. Its early
goals: greater share in government for educated Indians, using "constitutional
methods."

[info title="The safety-valve debate"]
Lala Lajpat Rai and later R. P. Dutt argued Hume created the Congress as a
"safety valve" to vent discontent before it exploded; others call Hume a
"lightning conductor" for genuine nationalism. Either way, Indians seized
the platform — Dadabhai Naoroji, the "Grand Old Man," presided three times.
[/info]

## 3. The Moderates (1885-1905)

The Moderates — **Naoroji, G. K. Gokhale, Ranade, R. C. Dutt** — worked by
petition, speech, and council vote. Their weapon was the **economic
critique** of colonialism: Naoroji's *Poverty and Un-British Rule in India*
and R. C. Dutt's *Economic History of India* proved the **drain of wealth**
— home charges, and exports without returns — kept India poor. Small wins
followed (the Indian Councils Act 1892), but critics mocked the method as
"political mendicancy."

## 4. The Extremists and the Swadeshi Storm

@diagram tree id="congress-streams" layout="tree" aria="The Congress divided into Moderate and Extremist streams"
  Indian National Congress -> "Moderates"
  Indian National Congress -> "Extremists"
  Moderates -> "Naoroji, Gokhale, Ranade: petitions and critique"
  Extremists -> "Lal-Bal-Pal and Aurobindo: swadeshi and boycott"
  Extremists -> "Goal: Swaraj as a birthright"
@/diagram

| Aspect | Moderates | Extremists |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Leaders | Naoroji, Gokhale, Ranade | Tilak, Lajpat Rai, Bipin Chandra Pal, Aurobindo |
| Method | Petitions, council work | Swadeshi, boycott, national education |
| Base | English-educated professionals | Students, small towns |
| Goal | Reform within empire | Swaraj — Tilak: "my birthright" |

Curzon's **partition of Bengal (16 October 1905)** — splitting the province
on communal-administrative lines — ignited the **Swadeshi movement**:
boycott of British cloth and schools, Rabindranath Tagore's *Amar Sonar
Bangla*, and Aurobindo's Bengal National College. The **Surat Split
(December 1907)** broke the Congress over the presidency; the reunion at
**Lucknow (1916)** returned the Extremists.

[important title="Partition annulled"]
The partition that was meant to weaken Bengali nationalism was itself
annulled in **1911** — the first great victory of mass agitation in
British India, and proof that pressure worked.
[/important]

## 5. The Revolutionary Stream

Secret societies answered repression with the bomb and pistol: the
**Chapekar brothers** (1897) killed Rand at Poona; **Khudiram Bose** was
hanged in 1908 for the Muzaffarpur attempt; **Aurobindo** was tried (and
acquitted) in the Alipore case. Abroad, **Shyamji Krishna Varma's** India
House in London and **Madam Bhikaji Cama** — who unfurled an early national
flag at Stuttgart in 1907 — carried the message to Europe, while the
**Ghadar Party** was founded in **San Francisco in 1913** by **Lala Har
Dayal** and Sohan Singh Bhakna, drawing Punjabi emigrants to plan a rising
during the First World War (the **Komagata Maru** affair of 1914 fed the
fury).

## 6. The Muslim League and the Morley-Minto Reforms

A deputation led by the **Aga Khan** met Viceroy Minto at **Simla (1906)**
asking for separate electorates; that December the **All-India Muslim
League** was founded at Dacca (Nawab Salimullah's initiative). The **Indian
Councils Act of 1909 (Morley-Minto reforms)** legalised **separate
electorates** — Minto is remembered as the father of the communal
electorate, the constitutional seed of partition.

## 7. The War Years: Home Rule and Lucknow

- **Tilak's Home Rule League** (April 1916, Poona) and **Annie Besant's**
  (September 1916, Madras) demanded self-government inside the empire,
  filling the void after the Surat Split.
- The **Lucknow Pact (December 1916)**: Congress and the League united on a
  common scheme of reforms — Congress accepted separate electorates, Jinnah
  bridging the two — the high point of Hindu-Muslim political cooperation.
- **Edwin Montagu's Declaration (August 1917)** promised "responsible
  government" as the goal, in return for war support.

[warning]
The Rowlatt Act of March 1919 — detention without trial, justified by
wartime emergency powers kept in peacetime — broke faith with the promise
of reform. Gandhi's nationwide hartal of 6 April 1919 opened the Gandhian
age and this lesson's story's sequel.
[/warning]

## Key Takeaways

- Pre-Congress associations created the platform; Hume's Congress (1885,
  Bonnerjee presiding) gave it a national roof.
- Moderates built the economic case against empire (drain theory);
  Extremists converted it into Swadeshi mass politics after the 1905
  Bengal partition.
- Revolutionaries at home (Khudiram, Aurobindo) and abroad (Ghadar, 1913)
  kept armed pressure alive.
- Separate electorates (1909), the Lucknow Pact (1916), and Home Rule
  leagues set the stage — and Rowlatt (1919) lit the Gandhian fuse.
